<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kantipur</h1><p class="st-meta">1484 CE – 1768 CE · capital Kathmandu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Malla kingdom of Kathmandu, whose rulers built much of Hanuman Dhoka and Kathmandu Durbar Square before the Gorkha conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kantipur emerged in 1484 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratna-malla">Ratna Malla</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaksha-malla">Yaksha Malla</a> of the Malla dynasty, established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> after his father's death. It became one of the three rival Malla kingdoms of the valley, alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a> and, from the late 16th century, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lalitpur">Kingdom of Lalitpur</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla">Pratap Malla</a>, in the mid 17th century, was the most celebrated king: a poet and builder who added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-dhoka">Hanuman Dhoka</a> palace and set up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rani">Rani</a> Pokhari tank. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> profited from trade with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>, minting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar</a> artisans produced fine metal images and painted scrolls, and the cult of the living goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumari-goddess">Kumari</a> was associated with the court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-jatra">Indra Jatra</a> festival in 1768, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaya-prakash-malla">Jaya Prakash Malla</a> fled. Kathmandu became the capital of the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kantipur.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kantipur" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: E. H. Walsh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Jaya_Prakash_Malla,_1753_CE,_Newar_script.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1484 CE – 1768 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kathmandu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ratna Malla, Pratap Malla, Jaya Prakash Malla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Newar Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Newari (Nepal Bhasa)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nepal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Kathmandu, Kantipur Malla kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
